1.  Purpose

This runbook is the step-by-step operational guide for every developer and engineer working with the TDCJ GitHub organization and Azure deployment pipeline. Follow this runbook for every infrastructure change. No exceptions.

2.  GitHub Organization Standards (Revise this based on Richard B recommendations)

Standard	Requirement
Organization Name	TDCJ (or TDCJ-Cloud — to be confirmed with GitHub admin)
Organization Owner	
Repository Naming	tdcj-[workload]-[component] — e.g. tdcj-cjis-infrastructure, tdcj-shared-networking
Default Branch	main — all production deployments originate from main
Branch Protection	Required: PR before merge, at least 1 approval, dismiss stale reviews, require status checks to pass
Admins	Cloud Governance Lead only — no one else has admin rights to the org
Access	Developers: Write to feature branches only. Cloud Engineers: Write + review. Gov. Lead: Admin.

3.  Repository Structure Standard
A recommended approach based on industry best practices but not an enforceable structure

Every workload repository must follow this folder structure:
/modules                         (shared reusable Bicep modules)
  /network                        (VNet, NSG, subnet modules)
  /compute                        (VM, App Service modules)
  /data                           (SQL, Storage Account modules)
  /security                       (Key Vault, identity modules)
/workloads
  /[workload-name]
    main.bicep                    (workload-level deployment)
    /params
      dev.bicepparam               (dev environment parameters)
      test.bicepparam              (test environment parameters)
      stg.bicepparam               (staging parameters)
      prod.bicepparam              (production parameters)
/.github
  /workflows
    deploy.yml                    (main pipeline definition)
    pr-validation.yml             (PR validation — what-if + lint)

4.  Step-by-Step: Making a Change

Step 1 — Create a feature branch
Never work directly on main. Always create a feature branch first.
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b feature/[your-change-description]
Branch naming: feature/, fix/, chore/ prefix. Use kebab-case. Example: feature/add-cjis-keyvault

Step 2 — Write your Bicep code
•	Use approved modules from /modules wherever possible — do not copy and paste module code into workload files
•	Never hardcode secrets, passwords, or connection strings — reference Key Vault using the existing() function
•	Every resource must include all four required tags: Environment, Owner, CostCenter, Project
•	Follow the naming convention: TDCJ-Platform-Environment-Region-Service-Purpose-Instance

Step 3 — Validate your Bicep locally
az bicep build --file main.bicep
Fix all linting errors before opening a PR. The pipeline will fail if linting errors exist.

Step 4 — Open a Pull Request
Push your branch and open a PR targeting main. Fill in every field of the PR template:
•	Change Classification: Standard / Normal / Significant / Emergency
•	What is changing and why: clear description of the resources being added/modified/removed
•	What-if output: the pipeline runs what-if automatically — confirm the output matches your intent
•	Rollback plan: how to reverse this change if it causes issues
•	Testing evidence: confirmation that the change was tested in dev before requesting production approval

Step 5 — Pipeline runs automatically
On PR open, the pipeline automatically runs:
1.	Bicep lint — fails if any linting errors
2.	What-if analysis — shows exact resource changes
3.	Security scan — flags any credentials committed to code
4.	Tag validation — confirms all required tags are present
Review the what-if output carefully. If it shows more changes than you expected — investigate before requesting review.

Step 6 — Request review
Assign the Cloud Governance Lead as reviewer. The PR cannot be merged without at least one approval. Add a comment summarizing the what-if output and confirming it matches your intent.

Step 7 — Pipeline deploys to dev
On PR merge approval, the pipeline automatically deploys to dev. Validate that the resource exists and behaves correctly in dev before proceeding.

Step 8 — Approve production gate
The pipeline pauses at the production gate. The designated approver (Cloud Governance Lead for Normal changes, IT Director for Significant changes) explicitly approves. Production deployment begins only after this approval.

Step 9 — Pipeline deploys to production
The pipeline deploys to production automatically after gate approval. Monitor the deployment in GitHub Actions. A failed production deployment triggers an immediate alert to the Cloud Governance Lead.

Step 10 — Post-deployment validation
Validate the change in production within 30 minutes of deployment. Add a comment to the PR confirming validation. The PR is closed after successful validation.

5.  Emergency Break-Glass Process

Use this process ONLY for active production incidents requiring immediate action. Misusing break-glass for convenience is a governance violation.

5.	Contact Cloud Governance Lead by phone or Teams — same hour, no waiting
6.	Gov. Lead contacts IT Director for verbal authorization
7.	Gov. Lead documents the emergency in the exception register before or within 1 hour of the action
8.	Engineer takes the minimum required emergency action
9.	Engineer opens a backfill PR within 24 hours capturing the change in Bicep
10.	Backfill PR links to the exception register entry
11.	Gov. Lead reviews at next governance board meeting

6.  Common Mistakes to Avoid

Mistake	Why It Matters	Correct Approach
Working directly on main branch	Bypasses PR review and branch protection — no approval trail	Always create a feature branch. main accepts merges only.
Hardcoding secrets in Bicep	Secrets in Git are permanent — a security incident	Use Key Vault existing() reference. Never put passwords in parameters.
Skipping the PR template fields	Incomplete PRs fail the pipeline and block deployment	Fill in every field. Change classification is required.
Copying module code instead of referencing	Creates drift and maintenance issues across workloads	Use module reference in /modules. Never copy module content.
Fixing resources in the portal instead of IaC	Creates drift between IaC and actual environment	Fix in Bicep, open PR, deploy through pipeline. Portal is read-only for production.

